## Lore

Shale Bogles are small, chalk-white D-Bees who live in shale beds, rock
quarries, old tunnel systems and abandoned mines in the northeast of the old
American Empire. Their bone-white skin stretched over a skinny frame, bald
heads, huge dark eyes and jagged, grinning mouths make outsiders take them for
ghosts or goblins, a reputation the name "bogle" does nothing to help. In truth
they are flesh-and-blood people, and fairly gentle ones.

A Bogle village, a "Bogle Hole", is a warren of shallow, narrow and flimsy
tunnels stacked on top of each other. The poor construction is on purpose:
larger beings fear a cave-in and stay out, while the Bogles scamper through,
dig through loose dirt like moles and need very little air if a tunnel does
collapse.

Most Bogles live a near Stone Age life of farming, gathering and foraging for
grubs, insects and small animals. They love sweet things such as honey, jam,
candy and wine, which they cannot make themselves and so trade for or pilfer.
They sneak into travelers'' camps at night to take food and simple gear,
believing that intruders in their territory are fair game, but they steal
rather than attack, and flee giggling into the nearest hole when caught. They
have even been known to help the lost and to care for abandoned children.

Each village is a tribe ruled by a male chief chosen by the female elders for
two years, with two advisors. Bogles are nocturnal, shy and secretive with
strangers, yet curious about surface folk and oddly drawn to humans, Quick-Flex
Aliens and Kraks. Only a few ever leave home to seek adventure in the wider
world.

## GM Notes

Also known as Shale Ghosts, Shale Goblins, Quarry Goblins and Dirt Ghosts;
they call themselves "the people". Size 4-5 feet (1.2 to 1.5 m). Weight 90-130
pounds (40.5 to 58.5 kg). Life span 2D6+44 years; mature at 13, one young after
an eight month pregnancy.

Standard equipment for a male hunter/defender or a Bogle who goes
adventuring: one weapon for every W.P., 1D4 E-Clips as appropriate, a suit of
patchwork armor (4D6+28 M.D.C.), a folding shovel, a small sack, a large sack, a
backpack, a blanket, a water skin or canteen, tinted goggles and basic
traveling clothes.

Habitat: temperate climates, most often Pennsylvania, New York, West Virginia,
Kentucky and Tennessee. A rural village has 2D6+10 residents, a large one up to
6D6+20; several thousand are rumored to live in Alaska. Slave market value
2D4x1,000 as unskilled labor and mine workers.

Allies: none as such, but they get along well with humans, Grackle Tooth,
Hobgoblins, Kraks, Larmac, Quick-Flex Aliens and Vanguard Brawlers. Enemies:
none as such; other nocturnal and subterranean beings may be rivals, and they
fear aggressive, violent people such as Psi-Goblins.
